<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

$q = static fn(string $difficulty, string $stem, string $answer, array $distractors, string $explanation, string $competency, string $scenario): array => [
    'difficulty'=>$difficulty, 'question'=>$stem, 'answer'=>$answer, 'distractors'=>$distractors,
    'explanation'=>$explanation, 'tested_competency'=>$competency, 'scenario'=>$scenario,
];

return [
    'sne-udl-flexible-engagement-representation-expression'=>[
        $q('easy','A lesson objective asks learners to explain how a plant grows, but the only resource is dense printed text. Which design choice best anticipates learner variability?','Pair clear text with a labeled diagram or oral explanation and allow learners to show understanding in an aligned format.',['Keep the printed text unchanged and provide help only after a learner fails.','Replace the plant-growth objective with a coloring task for the entire class.','Let learners choose any activity, even when it does not address the objective.'],'UDL anticipates access barriers through purposeful options while keeping the shared learning goal visible.','Design multiple routes to information and aligned expression.','plant-growth-materials'),
        $q('medium','A history assessment requires an oral presentation, though the stated outcome is to compare two sources. Which revision is strongest?','Allow a written comparison, recorded explanation, or structured visual response if each shows the same source-comparison reasoning.',['Keep public speaking as an unstated requirement because it is easy to observe.','Remove the comparison requirement for learners who prefer another format.','Replace the source task with a participation score.'],'Multiple expression options can preserve the comparison competency when speaking is not itself the target.','Offer flexible action and expression aligned to the outcome.','history-source-comparison'),
        $q('medium','A teacher gives learners a choice between two equivalent texts and a teacher-read version, then asks all to identify the same main idea. What makes this a sound UDL decision?','The options vary access to information while preserving a common learning intention.',['The learners have different objectives because the materials are not identical.','The teacher is avoiding assessment because choice was offered.','The formats should be removed so everyone sees the same page.'],'UDL can vary representation without changing what the learner is expected to understand.','Design flexible representation for a shared goal.','equivalent-text-options'),
        $q('medium','During a long worksheet, several learners stop responding, but they engage during short problem demonstrations. What is a useful design response?','Break the task into clear chunks with brief checks and a meaningful choice of practice item.',['Increase the number of worksheet questions to build endurance.','Assume the learners do not value mathematics and reduce the objective.','Make the demonstration optional and keep the worksheet unchanged.'],'Chunking and checks address task design and support strategy while retaining the math goal.','Anticipate barriers in task length and support engagement.','worksheet-engagement'),
        $q('medium','A teacher uses captions, diagrams, and hands-on models for a concept but does not explain how they connect. What should be improved?','Explicitly link the representations so learners can see the same idea across each form.',['Remove all but one representation to avoid learner choice.','Add more media without discussing relationships among them.','Assess only who can name the media used in class.'],'Multiple representations are most useful when learners can connect them to the underlying concept.','Connect different representations to build understanding.','representation-connections'),
        $q('hard','A learner says a digital response form is difficult to navigate and asks to use a familiar communication tool. What is the strongest next step?','Check whether the familiar tool can provide valid evidence of the objective and arrange access through the learner’s plan.',['Require the digital form because all learners received the same link.','Assume the learner’s preferred tool is unsuitable without checking the objective.','Remove the learner from the assessment until the system is changed.'],'Learner input is evidence about access; evaluate the tool against the learning target and planned supports.','Use learner feedback to reduce a design barrier while preserving valid evidence.','digital-form-access'),
    ],
    'sne-accessible-communication-and-technology'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner uses an augmentative communication device to answer class questions. What should the teacher plan before a group discussion?','Ensure the device is available, charged, and has the vocabulary needed for the discussion.',['Ask the learner to leave the device with an aide to avoid distraction.','Wait until after the discussion to ask what the learner wanted to say.','Use only questions that can be answered with a yes or no.'],'Consistent access to the learner’s communication tool supports participation and meaningful expression.','Maintain access to communication supports.','aac-group-discussion'),
        $q('medium','A digital reading packet works with a screen reader only on some pages because headings are formatted inconsistently. What should the teacher do?','Provide a structured accessible file and check that navigation and reading order work with the learner’s tool.',['Ask the learner to use a peer’s notes instead of the packet.','Convert each page to an image so the layout looks identical.','Read only the first page aloud and omit the rest of the learning materials.'],'Accessible structure helps assistive technology convey content and navigation consistently.','Check digital material compatibility with assistive technology.','screen-reader-packet'),
        $q('medium','A learner communicates more slowly using a speech-generating device during class discussion. Which response best supports access?','Allow processing time and plan turn-taking so the learner can complete the intended contribution.',['Finish the learner’s message to keep the discussion moving.','Ask a nearby classmate to answer instead.','Skip the learner’s turn whenever the response takes longer than peers.'],'The learner needs time to communicate; pacing and turn-taking can support participation without substituting another person’s answer.','Respect communication time and retain learner agency.','device-response-time'),
        $q('medium','A student with low vision receives a digital worksheet. Which check is most useful before the lesson?','Ask the student which display features help and test the file with the student’s usual access method.',['Assume magnification will work because the file is digital.','Print the same worksheet in the same font for everyone.','Replace the worksheet with an unrelated listening activity.'],'Digital format alone does not guarantee access; check compatibility with the learner’s actual tools and preferences.','Match accessible technology to learner and material.','low-vision-digital-file'),
        $q('medium','A communication board contains words needed for the science lesson but is stored in another room. What is the best preparation?','Place the learner’s communication support where it is available during the science activity.',['Bring it only for formal assessment so it is not overused.','Ask the learner to memorize the science vocabulary instead.','Use a classmate as the learner’s permanent interpreter.'],'A support cannot enable communication if it is not available at the moment the learner needs it.','Ensure assistive communication is accessible in context.','communication-board-location'),
        $q('hard','A learner uses a switch to select answers. The assessment interface requires dragging items with a mouse. What should the teacher do?','Adapt the response interface or provide an equivalent selection method and verify it still measures the intended skill.',['Score the learner on mouse speed because the interface is standardized.','Ask another learner to operate the mouse and choose the answers.','Cancel the assessment without seeking an accessible response option.'],'An interface barrier unrelated to the target should be addressed while preserving learner control and valid evidence.','Align assistive access with the assessed competency.','switch-access-assessment'),
    ],
    'sne-assessment-accommodation-validity'=>[
        $q('easy','A science quiz assesses understanding of water-cycle stages, but the printed diagram is too small for a learner to access. Which support is most appropriate?','Provide an accessible enlarged or digital diagram while keeping the same water-cycle reasoning task.',['Replace the quiz with an unrelated activity about classroom rules.','Score only the learner’s handwriting rather than the science response.','Ask a classmate to choose the stages for the learner.'],'The diagram access barrier can be removed without changing the science construct or another person answering.','Preserve the assessed competency while improving access.','science-diagram-access'),
        $q('medium','An assessment measures map-reading but requires extensive copying of labels. A learner can identify locations accurately by pointing. What is the strongest decision?','Permit pointing or a prepared marker response if it still reveals the learner’s map-reading knowledge.',['Keep copying as part of the test even if writing is not in the map-reading objective.','Give the learner the answer key because the copying is difficult.','Change the target to coloring rather than location identification.'],'An alternate response mode may preserve the map-reading construct when handwriting is incidental.','Distinguish the target skill from an unrelated output demand.','map-reading-response'),
        $q('medium','A teacher allows a communication device during an assessment of a learner’s ideas. What should the teacher verify?','That the device supports the learner’s response and does not supply the content being assessed.',['That the device is removed before scoring so answers look uniform.','That a support person chooses the ideas on the learner’s behalf.','That the device changes the learning objective to technology use.'],'The support should provide access to expression while the learner supplies the evidence for the target.','Use an accommodation without compromising construct validity.','device-and-idea-assessment'),
        $q('medium','A learner receives additional processing time on a reading-comprehension task. Which interpretation is most appropriate?','The teacher can interpret comprehension evidence while noting the assessment condition and applicable plan.',['The result is automatically invalid because time was adjusted.','The added time proves the learner has mastered every reading skill.','The teacher should compare the learner only with scores from untimed tests.'],'Time can be an access condition; interpretation should consider the target and document how evidence was obtained.','Interpret accommodated evidence within its conditions.','processing-time-reading'),
        $q('medium','A practical task assesses safe tool handling. The learner is offered a verbal explanation instead of performing the handling steps. What must the teacher consider?','Whether the verbal response demonstrates the intended performance or changes the task from doing to describing.',['Whether the learner’s explanation sounds confident.','Whether all learners can submit a written report instead.','Whether the task can be removed from the assessment without review.'],'If the target is actual tool handling, explaining the procedure may not show the required performance.','Protect validity when selecting an alternate response.','tool-handling-construct'),
        $q('hard','A support makes a reading passage accessible but an item asks learners to decode the exact printed word aloud. What is the key assessment question?','Whether the support preserves access to the intended construct or changes the decoding demand named in the item.',['Whether the support is convenient for the teacher to administer.','Whether the learner has used the same support in another subject.','Whether every assessment must use identical materials.'],'An accommodation is appropriate only in relation to the construct; decoding itself may be the target in this item.','Analyze how an accommodation interacts with the assessed construct.','decoding-item-validity'),
    ],
    'sne-data-informed-individualized-planning'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner’s reading score is low on one unfamiliar passage, while class observations show strong understanding during oral discussion. What should guide an individualized planning meeting?','Review both sources and gather additional evidence about reading access and comprehension before selecting support.',['Use the single score as the complete description of the learner.','Ignore the score because oral discussion was stronger.','Choose a support based only on the learner’s grade level.'],'Multiple sources can reveal whether the barrier is decoding, access, context, or comprehension; more evidence supports sound planning.','Use comprehensive data rather than a single score.','mixed-reading-evidence'),
        $q('medium','A team is planning support for a learner who wants to read short instructions independently. Which goal is most useful?','State an observable reading action, the conditions or material, and how progress will be checked.',['Write “improve reading” without a measurable behavior or review point.','Set a goal that the learner will work harder than classmates.','Choose a support before identifying the reading task.'],'A functional goal connects the learner’s priority to observable evidence and planned support.','Write measurable learner-centered goals.','independent-instructions-goal'),
        $q('medium','Assessment and family input both indicate that a learner participates more when instructions are broken into steps. What should the plan specify?','The step format, when staff will use it, and what evidence will show whether it improves participation.',['That the learner should receive help whenever an adult remembers.','That the family should deliver all classroom directions.','That the learner should stop attempting multi-step work.'],'A useful plan names the support, responsibility, and evidence for review.','Translate evidence into a defined support and monitoring plan.','stepwise-instructions-plan'),
        $q('medium','A learner meets a goal during one-to-one work but not during group work. What should the team examine?','Differences in group directions, timing, noise, peer interaction, and available supports across settings.',['Whether the learner should lose access to group work.','Whether one-to-one success proves the group result is irrelevant.','Whether the learner should repeat the same one-to-one task indefinitely.'],'Performance varies with setting; examine those conditions before deciding what support is needed.','Use context data to plan generalization and access.','one-to-one-group-difference'),
        $q('medium','A learner is highly motivated by a hands-on science task but has difficulty with lengthy written directions. Which plan is strongest?','Use the learner’s strength to introduce the science task and make the directions accessible in clear, manageable steps.',['Replace science learning with a handwriting intervention.','Remove the hands-on task because the directions are difficult.','Use the same long directions and wait for the learner to request help.'],'Planning can build on learner strengths while addressing the specific access barrier.','Use strengths and targeted supports together.','science-strength-directions'),
        $q('hard','A support has been used consistently for several weeks, but repeated goal-aligned data show no change in participation. What should the team do?','Use the evidence and learner input to revise the support, then monitor again.',['Keep the support unchanged because it is written in the plan.','Conclude the learner is unwilling to learn.','Remove the goal without reviewing the evidence.'],'Lack of progress is a reason for collaborative review and adjustment, not blame or unexamined continuation. Confirm how the support was implemented, consider barriers and learner perspective, then document the next plan.','Use repeated progress evidence to revise individualized support.','no-progress-review'),
    ],
    'sne-collaborative-learner-family-planning'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner explains that written instructions are hard to follow but pictures help. How should this information enter planning?','Record the learner’s view and check which visual support helps with the actual classroom tasks.',['Dismiss the view until a standardized assessment confirms it.','Ask the learner’s family to decide without the learner’s input.','Use pictures in every task without checking their fit.'],'Learner voice is useful planning evidence, and the support should be tested against the learning context.','Include learner input and evaluate a proposed support.','learner-visual-support'),
        $q('medium','A family reports that a learner uses a schedule successfully at home. What is the most productive school response?','Ask how the schedule is used and explore whether an adapted version could support school routines.',['Require the family to provide the same schedule materials for every class.','Assume the home schedule will work unchanged in every school setting.','Ignore the information because home practice is unrelated to learning.'],'Family knowledge can inform a school support, but the team should adapt and evaluate it in context.','Use family expertise while checking contextual fit.','home-schedule'),
        $q('medium','A speech-language specialist recommends a communication support for classroom discussion. What is the teacher’s role?','Coordinate classroom use with the specialist and learner, then observe whether it supports participation.',['Diagnose the learner’s communication condition independently.','Leave the support unused because it was not chosen by the teacher.','Ask the specialist to lead every classroom discussion.'],'The teacher collaborates on classroom implementation while staying within professional roles.','Coordinate support with qualified team members.','specialist-recommendation'),
        $q('medium','A team is creating a behavior support plan. Which information should be brought to the discussion?','Observable patterns across situations, the learner’s communication and strengths, and input from people who know the context.',['A list of character labels assigned by several adults.','Only the most recent incident without any context.','A consequence selected before identifying what the learner needs.'],'Comprehensive contextual evidence supports an individualized positive plan; labels do not explain the situation.','Bring relevant evidence and learner context to collaborative planning.','behavior-plan-evidence'),
        $q('medium','Two teachers disagree about whether a visual checklist helps a learner begin a task. What is the best way to resolve the disagreement?','Agree on a brief observation plan and compare task-start evidence with and without the checklist under appropriate conditions.',['Let the more senior teacher decide without gathering evidence.','Keep the checklist permanently because one teacher prefers it.','Ask the learner to choose which teacher is correct.'],'A shared, respectful evidence check can clarify whether the support improves the intended participation.','Use collaboration and observable evidence to evaluate support.','teacher-support-disagreement'),
        $q('hard','A new classroom team receives an old plan but no record of which supports the learner found useful. What should the team do first?','Review current information with the learner and family, confirm applicable plan roles, and observe supports in the new setting.',['Apply every old support exactly as written without checking current fit.','Discard the plan because the previous team has changed.','Ask the learner to select every service without team discussion.'],'Support should be continuous but responsive; current learner and family input plus observation helps the team confirm fit.','Coordinate continuity and reassess current learner needs.','new-team-handoff'),
    ],
    'sne-positive-behavior-support'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner leaves a seat during long verbal directions but remains engaged when instructions are shown in short steps. What should the teacher examine first?','Whether the direction format and task duration create barriers that can be adjusted.',['Whether the learner should lose access to the activity.','Whether leaving the seat proves the learner is choosing to misbehave.','Whether peers should be asked to keep the learner seated.'],'The difference across conditions suggests that task access and instruction may affect participation.','Examine environmental and instructional context before assigning intent.','verbal-directions-context'),
        $q('medium','A learner sometimes shouts when unable to get a turn with shared equipment. Which support is most constructive?','Teach and practice a clear way to request a turn, then reinforce the communication when it occurs.',['Remove all shared equipment so the learner cannot request a turn.','Use public reprimands each time the learner calls out.','Wait for shouting to stop without providing another communication option.'],'Teaching a replacement communication response addresses participation needs and avoids punitive escalation.','Teach a positive replacement skill.','shared-equipment-turn'),
        $q('medium','A staff member proposes an aversive technique to stop a learner’s repeated calling out. What is the appropriate professional response?','Use positive, evidence-based support and follow the applicable learner plan and school procedures.',['Try the technique briefly before notifying the team.','Use it only when other learners are watching.','Make it part of the classroom routine for everyone.'],'The current PRC TOS calls for positive behavior support and excludes aversive techniques.','Apply positive support and respect the current professional scope.','aversive-technique-proposal'),
        $q('medium','A learner asks for help by pushing materials away because the help symbol is not within reach. Which adjustment is useful?','Make the communication support available and teach or prompt its use during the task.',['Remove the materials whenever the learner pushes them away.','Wait for the learner to use spoken words before providing help.','Ask another student to decide whether the learner needs assistance.'],'Access to a communication response can replace a behavior that currently signals difficulty.','Make communication access part of positive support.','help-request-symbol'),
        $q('medium','A learner’s participation drops when a task changes without warning. What is the strongest support to try?','Use a predictable transition cue and a short preview, then observe whether engagement improves.',['Increase surprise changes so the learner becomes accustomed to them.','Remove the next task without explaining what follows.','Treat reduced participation as evidence of low motivation.'],'A clear preview may reduce an avoidable transition barrier; monitor its effect.','Use antecedent information to adjust the environment.','unannounced-task-change'),
        $q('hard','A learner repeatedly taps materials during independent work, but the behavior stops during hands-on tasks with similar content. What is a sound next step?','Compare task demands and access conditions, then plan a supportive alternative while collecting further observations.',['Conclude the learner is intentionally disrupting every lesson.','Remove all materials to prevent tapping in any class.','Ignore the difference and apply the same consequence everywhere.'],'Different participation across task formats is evidence to examine context and function before choosing support.','Use contextual data to select positive individualized support.','tapping-materials'),
    ],
    'sne-environmental-access-and-participation'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner cannot reach frequently used classroom materials from the assigned seat. What is the most appropriate environmental change?','Place the materials within an accessible reach and check that the learner can use them independently or with planned support.',['Ask classmates to retrieve every item for the learner.','Move the materials farther away to keep the aisle clear.','Replace the hands-on task with a listening activity.'],'A change to layout can remove a physical barrier while keeping the learning task available.','Adjust the environment to support access and independence.','classroom-material-reach'),
        $q('medium','A student misses directions when multiple groups speak at once but follows them in a quieter setting. What should the teacher test?','Reduce competing sound during instructions and provide a clear visual or written cue.',['Increase the volume of all group conversations.','Give the same directions only once from a different room.','Remove group participation from the student’s schedule.'],'The setting difference points to a possible auditory access barrier; the teacher can modify conditions and check the outcome.','Identify an environmental access barrier and evaluate a support.','competing-sound-directions'),
        $q('medium','A learner using a mobility device cannot enter a project group because desks block the route. What should the teacher do?','Rearrange the desks to create a usable route and shared workspace for the group.',['Ask the learner to work separately because the groups are already formed.','Move the learner’s materials while leaving the blocked route unchanged.','Ask peers to carry the learner’s device into the group.'],'The barrier is in the room arrangement; changing it supports shared participation without separating the learner.','Remove a physical barrier to group participation.','blocked-group-route'),
        $q('medium','A science activity uses spoken directions, but learners cannot revisit the steps during independent work. Which change improves access?','Provide concise written or visual steps that remain available while learners work.',['Repeat all directions more quickly before distributing equipment.','Expect learners to remember every step after one explanation.','Replace independent work with teacher demonstration only.'],'Persistent directions can support recall and independent participation without changing the science task.','Make key instructions accessible during the activity.','science-step-reference'),
        $q('medium','A learner is assigned a peer helper in every task, even when the learner can complete it independently. What should the teacher reconsider?','Whether the support is necessary for the specific task and whether it limits independence or peer reciprocity.',['Whether the helper should make more decisions for the learner.','Whether the learner should always work alone.','Whether all learners should receive a permanent helper.'],'Support should remove barriers and preserve agency; an unnecessary helper can create dependence or social imbalance.','Match assistance to the actual access need.','permanent-peer-helper'),
        $q('hard','A learner participates in group discussion when given a preview of key vocabulary but not when new terms are introduced without context. Which plan is strongest?','Preview relevant vocabulary with accessible examples and check whether the learner can contribute to the same discussion goal.',['Give the learner a different discussion topic each time.','Remove new vocabulary from the lesson entirely.','Wait until the learner asks for every unfamiliar word.'],'A planned vocabulary preview can reduce a barrier while retaining the shared discussion and its content.','Use a targeted environmental/instructional support to enable access.','vocabulary-preview'),
    ],
    'sne-progress-monitoring-and-adjustment'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner’s goal is to begin a writing task using a visual checklist. Which evidence best monitors progress?','Record whether and how often the learner starts each step with the checklist across planned opportunities.',['Record whether the learner seemed cooperative that day.','Compare the learner’s handwriting with classmates’ work.','Ask only whether the checklist looks attractive.'],'The measure is observable and directly connected to the task-start goal.','Collect repeated goal-aligned evidence.','checklist-task-start'),
        $q('medium','A learner’s weekly data show steady improvement in reading accuracy but not comprehension. What should the teacher do?','Review which comprehension supports and questions are being used, then adjust instruction and monitor comprehension separately.',['Conclude that reading instruction is successful in every area.','Change the accuracy goal because the learner is improving.','Stop collecting data until the next formal assessment.'],'Separate measures show different progress patterns; instruction should respond to the area still needing support.','Use domain-specific evidence to make a targeted adjustment.','accuracy-comprehension-data'),
        $q('medium','A single end-of-term score is available for a learner’s participation goal. What additional information would improve a decision about support?','Repeated observations from different tasks and settings using the same participation definition.',['A comparison of the learner’s score with a class popularity rating.','A new goal chosen without reviewing the current one.','A report of whether the learner appeared cheerful.'],'Repeated observations reveal change and context more reliably than one isolated score.','Monitor progress over time and across relevant contexts.','single-term-score'),
        $q('medium','A learner uses a new self-advocacy phrase successfully with one teacher but not with other adults. What is a useful next step?','Practice the phrase across planned settings and check which conditions help the learner generalize it.',['Conclude the skill is mastered because it worked once.','Stop using the phrase so the learner becomes independent.','Require the learner to use it in every setting without support.'],'Transfer to other settings requires practice and evidence; the team can plan gradual supported generalization.','Monitor generalization and adjust support.','self-advocacy-generalization'),
        $q('medium','A learner wants to track progress toward completing a multi-step project. Which tool best supports self-monitoring?','A short learner-accessible checklist with steps the learner can mark and review with the teacher.',['An adult-only spreadsheet that the learner cannot see.','A class leaderboard showing who completes work fastest.','A long narrative report written at the end of the term.'],'An accessible checklist lets the learner participate in monitoring and planning next actions.','Build learner agency through usable progress tools.','project-self-monitoring'),
        $q('hard','A goal is marked “good classroom behavior,” and staff disagree about whether progress occurred. What is the best improvement?','Rewrite the goal with a specific observable participation behavior and define how it will be recorded.',['Choose one staff member’s judgment as the only acceptable measure.','Keep the wording and collect more impressions from adults.','Replace the goal with a consequence for any disagreement.'],'An observable definition makes evidence comparable and supports a meaningful review.','Clarify the target and measurement before judging progress.','vague-progress-goal'),
    ],
    'sne-dignity-choice-and-learner-voice'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner’s support plan is being discussed in front of classmates. What should the teacher do?','Move the discussion to an appropriate private setting and share only information needed for support.',['Continue because classmates may be curious about the plan.','Ask the learner to explain the plan to the group.','Post the plan so substitute teachers can find it easily.'],'Learner privacy and dignity require appropriate confidentiality and limited sharing.','Protect privacy during support planning.','public-plan-discussion'),
        $q('medium','A learner is offered two accessible ways to complete a project and chooses one. What should the teacher do next?','Confirm that the chosen method supports the learning goal and let the learner proceed with planned support.',['Replace the choice with the teacher’s preferred method.','Assume the learner chose incorrectly because it differs from peers.','Remove the project because learner choice was offered.'],'Choice supports agency when the option remains aligned with the learning intention.','Respect learner choice within a valid learning goal.','project-response-choice'),
        $q('medium','A learner uses a communication device to answer, but an adult routinely responds for them. What should the teacher do?','Address the learner directly, allow response time, and use the adult as support only where needed.',['Continue speaking to the adult because it is faster.','Ask the adult to choose the learner’s preferred response.','Stop asking the learner questions during class.'],'The learner is the communicator; the teacher should preserve direct participation and agency.','Communicate with the learner rather than speaking over them.','device-user-agency'),
        $q('medium','A student declines to discuss a personal experience during a lesson on identity. Which response is most respectful?','Accept the choice and offer a different example or fictional scenario that meets the learning objective.',['Require the student to share because personal reflection is useful.','Ask peers to persuade the student to participate.','Remove the student from the lesson.'],'Learner voice includes the ability not to disclose private experience; another route can meet the objective.','Respect privacy while maintaining access to learning.','declined-personal-sharing'),
        $q('medium','A teacher is unsure which disability-related term a learner prefers. What is the best action?','Ask the learner or family respectfully and use the preferred language in the appropriate context.',['Choose a label based on what other staff use for different learners.','Avoid speaking with the learner about their preferences.','Use a euphemism that obscures the learner’s support needs.'],'Contemporary respectful practice uses the learner’s or family’s preference without assuming one term fits everyone.','Use respectful learner-informed terminology.','terminology-preference'),
        $q('hard','A learner requests a break during a challenging activity, and the request is part of the learner’s plan. Which response best balances dignity and participation?','Honor the planned break, clarify how the learner can rejoin, and review whether the task remains accessible.',['Deny the request until the learner completes the entire activity.','End the learner’s participation for the rest of the day.','Announce the request to the class as a behavior lesson.'],'The planned support respects communication and regulation while maintaining a clear, supportive route back to learning.','Support learner self-advocacy and return to participation.','planned-break-request'),
    ],
    'sne-transition-and-community-participation'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner is moving to a new classroom. What should the sending and receiving teachers coordinate?','Relevant learning goals, effective supports, access tools, and the learner’s transition priorities.',['Every private detail from the learner’s full school record.','Only the learner’s past difficulties without successful supports.','A promise that the new classroom will be exactly the same.'],'A purposeful handoff carries information needed for access and learning while respecting privacy and learner priorities.','Coordinate relevant transition information.','classroom-transition'),
        $q('medium','A learner says a transition goal matters because they want to join a community art club. What is the strongest planning response?','Discuss the learner’s goal, identify relevant skills or supports, and coordinate a practical opportunity to practice them.',['Replace the goal with a standard adult-selected target.','Assume the learner can join without exploring access needs.','Ask the family to handle all planning independently.'],'Learner-selected goals can guide coordinated practice and support community participation.','Use learner priorities in transition planning.','community-art-goal'),
        $q('medium','A new support person will work with a learner during a community-based activity. What should be clarified?','The learner’s communication preferences, task goals, safety procedures, and each person’s support role.',['Only the schedule and arrival time.','The learner’s diagnosis without the activity expectations.','That the support person should make all decisions to avoid delay.'],'Clear roles and relevant access information support safety and meaningful participation.','Prepare coordinated support for a transition activity.','community-activity-support'),
        $q('hard','A transition meeting includes multiple adults but no opportunity for the learner to express a preference. What should the team add?','An accessible way for the learner to share goals, choices, and support needs before the plan is finalized.',['A vote among the adults about what the learner will prefer.','A longer discussion after the learner leaves the meeting.','A fixed plan based only on previous year’s goals.'],'Learner voice is essential to individualized planning; communication should be made accessible.','Include the learner in transition decisions.','learner-transition-voice'),
    ],
];
